00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:24.070 align:start position:0%
a trip to barcelona wouldn't be complete  without a visit to la rambla  the soul of the city all year round  millions of tourists come to soak up its  irresistible charm  at the heart of the boulevard is the  world famous market hall  la bocaria welcome to la bocaria  market it's one of the best markets in  barcelona  the market is steeped in history dating  back to the 13th century  this is one of the many stalls selling  fruit vegetables fresh fish and of  course tapas  one of the oldest and most famous tapas  bars is pinocchio  owned by juan baien  you'll need luck on your side if you  want to get a seat here though most  people leave it up to juan to choose  their meal  he's an expert in traditional catalan  cuisine  my mother opened this business here in  la bocaria long ago  she passed the tradition on to me and i  managed the shop now with my son  we've had many opportunities to move but  la bocaria on la rambla  is my home  grew up here he's witnessed many changes  to the street over the years  but for him la rambla will always be the  place to see  and be seen  no matter where you're from in barcelona  everyone gathers on la rambla  and that makes it one of the most  beautiful places in the city  la rambla is around 1.3 kilometers long  and runs from the hustle and bustle of  plaza de catalunya all the way to the  port of barcelona  a statue of columbus stands at one end  the rest of the boulevard is split into  five  sections each with its own name that's  why it's often referred to as  las ramblas in the plural  juan bayern's favorite is la rambla  saint george's  also known as rambla de las flores for  the many flower stalls  this spot on la rambla is where people  were allowed to sell flowers for the  first time in catalonia  and this tradition lives on today in  these stalls  they represent a wonderful piece of  history  it's said la rambla was once a sandy  riverbed  for more than 400 years a wall separated  barcelona from the western district of  al raval  in the early 18th century the wall was  torn down and the tree-lined promenade  was built  the first flower stalls appeared in the  mid-19th century  and soon became a local landmark  as did the grand theatre del desail  which opened in 1847  the opera house was destroyed in a fire  around 20 years ago  it's now been repaired to its former  beauty  the history of the boulevard is also  reflected in the numerous street cafes  although most people here are now  tourists they stroll down the street  year round  unfortunately making it a gold mine for  pickpockets  but they also provide an audience for  the many street performers  i like the fact that it's green and open  and nice but it's very very busy  i think the street is beautiful it  reminds me of the chancellisse in paris  it's very authentic especially the small  market behind it  la bocaria it's fantastic  but for locals too la rambla is still  the showplace of important events  at the faun de caneletes thousands of  barcelona fc fans gather regularly to  celebrate their team success  this fountain is also one of juan baen's  favorite spots  the water has more to it than meets the  eye  this water is magic legend has it  that if you drink from the fountain  you'll stay in barcelona  forever  staying here forever with la rambla at  your feet sounds appealing  even after all these years the boulevard  has lost  none of its charm
 

